Beyond text-pattern constraints, HTML5 validates numeric and date ranges natively, and gives you an escape hatch for validation logic the built-in attributes can't express.

## min, max, and step

These apply to numeric and date/time input types (`number`, `range`, `date`, `time`, `month`, `week`, `datetime-local`).

```html
<label for="quantity">Quantity</label>
<input type="number" id="quantity" name="quantity" min="1" max="10" step="1">

<label for="event-date">Event date</label>
<input type="date" id="event-date" name="event-date" min="2026-01-01" max="2026-12-31">
```

`min` and `max` define the valid range (inclusive). `step` defines the increment the value must align to, relative to `min` (or relative to a type-specific default if `min` is omitted, e.g. `0` for most number fields). A `number` input with `min="0"` and `step="5"` only accepts 0, 5, 10, 15, and so on; entering 7 is flagged as invalid.

## The :valid and :invalid CSS Pseudo-Classes

The browser exposes a field's current validity through CSS, letting you style valid/invalid states without any JavaScript.

```css
input:invalid {
  border-color: red;
}
input:valid {
  border-color: green;
}
```

A common refinement is to only apply this styling once a field has actually been interacted with, using `:not(:placeholder-shown)` or a JavaScript-added class, so empty required fields don't show as red the instant the page loads.

## setCustomValidity for Logic Attributes Can't Express

Sometimes validation requires comparing two fields (like "confirm password must match password") or custom business rules that no built-in attribute covers. The `setCustomValidity()` JavaScript method lets you mark a field invalid with a custom message, which then participates in the same native validation and submission-blocking flow as `required` or `pattern`.

```html
<input type="password" id="password" name="password">
<input type="password" id="confirm" name="confirm">

<script>
  const confirmField = document.getElementById('confirm');
  confirmField.addEventListener('input', () => {
    const matches = confirmField.value === document.getElementById('password').value;
    confirmField.setCustomValidity(matches ? '' : 'Passwords must match');
  });
</script>
```

Passing an empty string clears the custom error, marking the field valid again; any non-empty string marks it invalid with that message. This still does not replace server-side validation - it only improves the client experience.

interviewQuestions.answer:
The Constraint Validation API is the browser's built-in system for checking form field values against declarative HTML attributes (`required`, `pattern`, `min`, `max`, `step`, `minlength`, `maxlength`) and exposing the result both through CSS and through JavaScript.

**CSS integration**: every form control automatically matches either the `:valid` or `:invalid` pseudo-class at all times, based on its current constraint state, with no JavaScript required.

```css
input:invalid { border-color: crimson; }
input:valid { border-color: seagreen; }
```

A common refinement is pairing this with `:not(:placeholder-shown)` or a `:user-invalid`-style approach so empty required fields don't flash red before the user has even had a chance to type anything.

**JavaScript integration - `setCustomValidity()`**: every form control has a `validity` object and a `setCustomValidity(message)` method. Calling it with a non-empty string manually marks the field invalid, with that message, and makes it participate in the exact same blocking behavior as a built-in constraint - submission is blocked and the custom message appears in the native validation bubble. Calling it with an empty string clears the custom error, returning the field to following its ordinary (attribute-based) validity state.

```js
const confirmInput = document.getElementById('confirm-password');
confirmInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
  const match = confirmInput.value === document.getElementById('password').value;
  confirmInput.setCustomValidity(match ? '' : 'Passwords do not match');
});
```

This is the mechanism that lets you express validation rules the declarative attributes cannot (cross-field comparisons, server-confirmed uniqueness checks, complex business logic), while still getting the browser's native submission-blocking and error-bubble UI for free, rather than building a completely separate custom validation UI from scratch.

**Why none of this ever replaces server-side validation**: every single piece of this system - `required`, `pattern`, CSS classes, even `setCustomValidity()` calls - executes entirely within the user's own browser, which the user fully controls. A request can bypass the browser form altogether (a direct HTTP call via curl, a modified replay of a captured request, a different, non-browser API client), and in those cases none of this validation logic runs at all. Client-side validation exists purely to give legitimate users fast, helpful feedback without a round trip to the server; it provides zero security or data-integrity guarantee on its own. Production systems must always re-validate every constraint - required fields, formats, ranges, and business rules - on the server, treating all incoming data as untrusted regardless of what the client-side form appeared to enforce.
